{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>And Moses implored.</b> Some say that וַיְחַל is of the root of וַתִּתְחַלְחַל — that trembling and shuddering seized him before the face of HaShem, that is, in the sense of anger, for he saw the greatness of the wrath of HaShem and was terrified by the dread of HaShem, and he said, <b>Why, HaShem, does Your anger burn</b> — for had You said to chastise them Yourself, as a man chastises his son, I would have held my peace; but what You said, to send against them a cruel angel of wrath and fury — why do You do thus to Your servants? For I have claims regarding this. The first is, <b>against Your people.</b> For You said to me, ״כִּי שִׁחֵת עַמְּךָ״ — and that means the mixed multitude; if so, why should Your anger burn against Your people, that is, Israel. The second claim is, <b>whom You brought out of the land of Egypt.</b> A land full of idols of idolatry, and it is no wonder about them so much if they were drawn after habit. The third claim is, <b>with great power</b> — against the attribute of justice, for according to the line of justice it would have been fitting to destroy them within the land of Egypt, as it says in Ezekiel (Ezekiel 20:13) ״וָאֹמַר לְכַלּוֹתָם בְּתוֹךְ אֶרֶץ מִצְרַיִם, וָאַעַשׂ לְמַעַן שְׁמִי״ and so forth; and after You brought them out with great power against the attribute of justice, if so they are already established before You as idolaters, and what is newly revealed now? The fourth claim is, <b>and with a strong hand</b> — for it is well known that Israel did not want to leave Egypt, as it says (Exodus 14:12) ״הֲלֹא זֶה הַדָּבָר אֲשֶׁר דִּבַּרְנוּ אֵלֶיךָ בְמִצְרַיִם לֵאמֹר חֲדַל מִמֶּנּוּ וְנַעַבְדָה אֶת מִצְרָיִם״. And it is written (Exodus 13:18) ״וַחֲמֻשִׁים עָלוּ בְנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״ — for only a fifth part went up, and four parts died during the three days of darkness, because they did not want to leave Egypt. And even those who left — You brought them out with a strong hand against their will, not with their consent, as it says (Exodus 13:9) ״כִּי בְּיָד חֲזָקָה הוֹצִיאֲךָ ה׳״. And since You brought them out from there, it is fitting for You to have compassion upon them, so that they should not say, He brought them out for evil — since they left at Your word." "And after he pleaded on behalf of Israel, he began to plead also on behalf of the mixed multitude and said, ״לָמָּה יֹאמְרוּ מִצְרַיִם לֵאמֹר בְּרָעָה הוֹצִיאָם לַהֲרֹג אוֹתָם בֶּהָרִים״ — for this does not apply to Israel at all, and why should such a thing come from His hand, blessed be He? Rather, it was regarding the mixed multitude that he said thus, for HaShem had already executed judgments in Egypt, but they were not revealed to the whole world, and therefore He brought out the mixed multitude in order to kill them on high mountains visible to every eye, so that the vengeance against them would be openly revealed and publicized. And ״לָמָּה יֹאמְרוּ מִצְרַיִם לֵאמֹר״ — for all generations, for thus they do to converts who come to take shelter under the wings of the Shekhinah, and desecration of the Name would result from this. And he said, ״שׁוּב מֵחֲרוֹן אַפֶּךָ״ and so forth — and there are two sides: regarding the mixed multitude he sought ״שׁוּב מֵחֲרוֹן אַפֶּךָ״ — that He not chastise them through a cruel angel of wrath and fury; and regarding Israel he added and said ״וְהִנָּחֵם עַל הָרָעָה לְעַמֶּךָ״ — that He do them no evil at all, since they were not the cause of the sin. ״זְכֹר לְאַבְרָהָם״ and so forth — You say ״וְאֶעֶשְׂךָ לְגוֹי גָּדוֹל״; today or tomorrow perhaps my children will sin and You will do destruction to them — a fortiori from the Patriarchs, to whom You swore by Your Name! And moreover, they have the merit of the three Patriarchs, and all of this does not help; a fortiori for my children, concerning whom there is no oath but mere speech, and there is not even a single festival's seat. Immediately, ״וַיִּנָּחֶם ה׳ עַל הָרָעָה אֲשֶׁר דִּבֶּר לַעֲשׂוֹת לְעַמּוֹ״ — that is, what He had spoken to do, to destroy them utterly; but nevertheless He intended to chastise them with the rod of their transgression, as it says ״וּבְיוֹם פָּקְדִי וּפָקַדְתִּי עֲלֵיהֶם אֶת חַטָּאתָם״ — to exact payment from them little by little."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {1 "The phrase וְלִצְדָדִין קָתָנֵי ('and there are two sides') is rendered literally as 'and there are two sides,' preserving the Aramaic idiom; 'a single festival's seat' translates כִּסֵּא שֶׁל רֶגֶל אֶחָד hyper-literally, as the precise force of the idiom is uncertain."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:25:08.294499930Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}